## The details
### Resolves

Part of https://github.com/google/blockly/issues/7085.

### Proposed Changes

Introduce a `LuaGenerator` class to be the class of which `luaGenerator` is an instance (without any additional properties making it a singleton).
Introduce an `Order` enum (for now Closure-style) for the operator precedence table.
Don't rename `luaGenerator` to `Lua` in generators/lua/*.js.

#### Behaviour Before/After Change

There should be no changes in behaviour.

### Reason for Changes

See #7085.

### Test Coverage

Passes `npm test`; no changes to manual testing anticipated.

### Documentation

Documentation for the `LuaGenerator` class and `Order` enum should be automatically generated by our JSDoc configs.

### Additional Information

It is now possible to create multiple `luaGenerator` objects, which can share (or not share) block generator implementations.  Note that new instances do not by default come with any generator functions pre-installed.

```JS
import {luaGenerator, LuaGenerator} from 'blockly/lua';

const customJSGenerator = new LuaGenerator();

// Optionally copy standard generator functions to new instance:
Object.assign(customLuaGenerator.forBlock, luaGenerator.forBlock);

// Optionally add/replace some/all generator functions:
customLuaGenerator.forBlock('block_type') = function(block) {/* new implementation */};

// Optionally change settings:
customLuaGenerator.INDENT = '\t';  // Use tabs to indent generated code.
```

We now provide an enum of the operator precedence values (order of operations) constants as a separate export:

```JS
import {luaGenerator, Order} from 'blockly/lua';

// Old:
luaGenerator.forBlock('answer_expression_block') = function(block) {
  const code = '42';
  return [code, luaGenerator.ORDER_ATOMIC]
}

// New:
luaGenerator.forBlock('answer_expression_block') = function(block) {
  const code = '42';
  return [code, Order.ATOMIC]
}
```

**DEPRECATION:** The `.ORDER_*` constants on `luaGenerator` are deprecated; they still exist but are no longer part of the documented API and will be removed in a future version of Blockly.  We recommend that you update your custom generator code to use the `Order` enum at your earliest convenience.

